  "response": "I’ll run the targeted scan, inspect the fixture setup, and cap its concurrent filesystem work.\n\n\nThe scan confirms “Concurrency Stampede” in `test/utils.ts`: “fan-out: Promise.all at line 38” over “collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)”. Every fixture file starts work immediately, so larger fixtures increase overlapping writes. Whether that exhausts file descriptors depends on the machine’s limits, existing descriptor use, and I/O timing.\n\nI’ll process batches of 10 and verify that all files finish before the CLI starts.\n\n\nThe bounded version passes four regression checks, including a 103-file fixture with a simulated 10-write limit. The scan no longer reports the fan-out finding. I’m checking that the regression catches the original code and that an existing CLI test still passes.\n\n\nFixed [test/utils.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-1a6v8m/test/utils.ts:37).\n\n`crimes scan test --format json` identified **`unbounded_async_fanout` — “Concurrency Stampede”**, corresponding to `crime_00024` in your report (`crime_00008` in the targeted scan). Its evidence states:\n\n> “fan-out: Promise.all at line 38”  \n> “collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)”\n\nThe async `map` starts all N file tasks immediately; `Promise.all` only waits for them. Awaiting `mkdir` sequences operations within each task, but does not limit concurrency across files. Larger fixtures therefore increase overlapping writes. Descriptor limits, existing descriptor usage, test parallelism, and I/O timing vary between machines, explaining intermittent CI failures despite local success.\n\nThe replacement processes every file in batches of 10:\n\n```ts\nconst fileNames = Object.keys(files)\nconst concurrency = 10\nfor (let i = 0; i < fileNames.length; i += concurrency) {\n  await Promise.all(\n    fileNames.slice(i, i + concurrency).map(async (name) => {\n      const filePath = path.resolve(testDir, name)\n      const parentDir = path.dirname(filePath)\n      await fsp.mkdir(parentDir, { recursive: true })\n      return fsp.writeFile(filePath, files[name], 'utf8')\n    }),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis caps concurrent file tasks at 10 per `run`, preserves error propagation, and finishes fixture creation before launching the CLI.\n\nVerified: five targeted tests passed, including a 103-file regression that fails with simulated `EMFILE` under the original implementation. Build, focused typecheck, and formatting passed; rescanning reports no unbounded fan-out finding.",
